### 4. ASSESSMENT PROFILES AND TECHNICAL REFERENCES

Create explicit assessment profiles for envelope calculations and rule evaluation.

A profile should specify:
- Applicable jurisdiction and building use where relevant.
- Selected calculation method and edition.
- Required inputs.
- Applicable assembly types.
- Units and boundary conditions.
- Whether the result is a simplified screening calculation or a standards-based assessment.
- Known exclusions and uncertainty.
- Any configured comparison thresholds.
- Relevant source and rule-set version.

Investigate official reference material where relevant:

**ISO 6946:2017 — Building components and building elements: thermal resistance and thermal transmittance calculation methods.**

Official reference:
https://www.iso.org/standard/65708.html

This standard's scope has specific exclusions, including windows and other glazed units, curtain walls, ground-coupled components, and components through which air is designed to permeate. Do not use its layer calculation as a universal method for every envelope component.

**ISO 52016-1:2017 — Energy needs for heating and cooling, internal temperatures, and heat loads.**

Official reference:
https://www.iso.org/standard/65696.html

This standard contains hourly or monthly calculation procedures with distinct input and model requirements. Do not claim ISO 52016-1 compliance simply because a simplified heat-transfer estimate has been implemented.

**ISO 13788:2012 — Hygrothermal performance, critical surface humidity, and interstitial condensation calculation methods.**

Official reference:
https://www.iso.org/standard/51615.html

Its simplified methods do not account for all important physical phenomena. Any implementation based on a simplified method must state its limitations and the conditions under which it applies.

**ANSI/ASHRAE Standard 160-2021 — Criteria for Moisture-Control Design Analysis in Buildings.**

Official ASHRAE reference:
https://www.ashrae.org/technical-resources/standards-and-guidelines/titles-purposes-and-scopes

Check applicable addenda, the selected edition, availability of the actual procedure, and whether the implementation satisfies the inputs and evaluation requirements. Merely citing this standard does not establish that a complete moisture-control design analysis has been performed.

**ASHRAE/IES Standard 90.1 — Energy Standard for Sites and Buildings Except Low-Rise Residential Buildings.**

Official reference:
https://www.ashrae.org/technical-resources/bookstore/standard-90-1

Use the applicable edition, addenda, jurisdiction, building scope, and approved compliance path. Do not automatically apply commercial-building provisions to residential buildings or assume that the most recent published edition is the locally adopted legal requirement.

**ISO/TR 5863:2025 — Integrative design of the building envelope.**

Official reference:
https://www.iso.org/standard/81766.html

This reference covers integrated envelope-design principles, including thermal performance, airtightness, moisture control, and related building considerations.

These sources are starting points, not proof of a software implementation. Verify the current applicable status, scope, and requirements before making an alignment or compliance claim. Do not reproduce substantial copyrighted standards text.

